.TH find "" "Command"
.SH NAME
find \- search for files satisfying a pattern
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBfind \fIdirectory ... \fB[\fIexpression ...\fB]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
find -- search for files satisfying a pattern
Usage:	find directory ... [ expression ... ]
Expression:
	-atime n	File has been accessed in 'n' days
	-ctime n	File's i-node has been changed in 'n' days
	-exec command	Command is successful (arg. '{}' is file name)
	-group gname	File belongs to group owner 'gname'
	-inum n		File has i-node number 'n'
	-links n	File has 'n' links to it
	-mtime n	File has been modified in 'n' days
	-name pattern	File name matches 'pattern' (shell conventions)
	-newer file	File has been modified since 'file'
	-nop		Always true; does nothing
	-ok command	Like '-exec', except it asks
	-perm octal	File permissions are 'octal'
	-print		Always true; prints current pathname
	-size n		File is 'n' blocks long
	-type c		File matches type ('c' may be [bcdfmp])
	-user uname	File belongs to 'uname'
	exp -a exp	Both expressions are true (also: exp exp)
	exp -o exp	One of the expressions is true
	! exp		Expression is false
	( exp )		Parentheses for grouping
If no expression is specified, '-print' is assumed.
.HE
.B find
descends each given
.IR directory ,
testing every file and subdirectory it finds against
.IR expression .
If
.I expression
is omitted, or specifies no
.BR \-print ,
.BR \-exec ,
or
.BR \-ok ,
.B find
prints the pathname of every file it visits, exactly as though
.B \-print
had been given at the end.
.PP
An
.I expression
is built from the primaries below, combined with
.B \-a
(logical and, also the default when two primaries are simply
written one after the other),
.B \-o
(logical or),
.B !
(negation), and parentheses for grouping;
.B \-a
binds tighter than
.BR \-o .
Each primary not only tests something about the file, but, in the
case of
.BR \-exec ,
.BR \-ok ,
and
.BR \-print ,
also has the side effect of running a command or printing the
pathname; evaluation stops as soon as the truth of an
.B \-a
or
.B \-o
expression is known, so a primary with a side effect to the right of
.B \-o
after a true left operand, or to the right of
.B \-a
after a false one, does not run.
.PP
A numeric argument
.I n
may be written plain, or prefixed with
.B +
(more than
.IR n )
or
.B \-
(fewer than
.IR n ).
.SH Primaries
.IP "\-\fBatime \fIn\fR" 0.8i
The file was last accessed
.I n
days ago.
.IP "\-\fBctime \fIn\fR"
The file's i-node was last changed
.I n
days ago.
.IP "\-\fBexec \fIcommand\fR"
Run
.IR command ,
which continues to collect arguments up to a
.B ;
argument (escaped from the shell); true if it exits with status
zero.
Any argument written
.B {}
is replaced by the current pathname.
.IP "\-\fBgroup \fIgname\fR"
The file's group owner is
.IR gname ,
or, if
.I gname
is numeric, the group number
.IR gname .
.IP "\-\fBinum \fIn\fR"
The file's i-number is
.IR n .
.IP "\-\fBlinks \fIn\fR"
The file has
.I n
links.
.IP "\-\fBmtime \fIn\fR"
The file was last modified
.I n
days ago.
.IP "\-\fBname \fIpattern\fR"
The last component of the file's pathname matches
.IR pattern ,
using the wildcard conventions of the shell
.BR sh .
.IP "\-\fBnewer \fIfile\fR"
The file has been modified more recently than
.IR file .
.IP \-\fBnop\fR
Always true; has no side effect.
.IP "\-\fBok \fIcommand\fR"
Like
.BR \-exec ,
but first prints the command and the current pathname and asks the
user; runs
.I command
only if the answer begins with
.BR y .
.IP "\-\fBperm \fIoctal\fR"
The file's permission bits equal
.IR octal ;
if
.I octal
begins with
.BR \- ,
match instead if all of the bits set in the (remaining) octal value
are set in the file's mode, allowing setuid, setgid, and the sticky
bit to be tested as well.
.IP "\-\fBprint\fR"
Always true; prints the current pathname.
.IP "\-\fBsize \fIn\fR"
The file is
.I n
512-byte blocks long, rounding up.
.IP "\-\fBtype \fIc\fR"
The file's type is
.IR c :
one of
.BR b ", " c ", " d ", " f ", " m ", or " p
for block special, character special, directory, ordinary file,
multiplexor file, or named pipe.
.IP "\-\fBuser \fIuname\fR"
The file's owner is
.IR uname ,
or, if
.I uname
is numeric, the user number
.IR uname .
.SH Examples
.DS
find / \-name core \-mtime +7 \-exec rm {} ;
.DE
removes every file named
.B core
in the whole file tree that has not been changed in a week.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chmod ,
.BR sh ,
.B test
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B find
complains and continues if a directory cannot be opened or read, or
a
.B \-newer
file does not exist; it exits nonzero only if it was invoked with a
bad expression or a starting
.I directory
does not exist or is not a directory.
